read: app:doctrine-trust~17883 | Why art thou cast down, O my soul
read: app:doctrine-trust~16853 | Retribution theology is man's, not God's
read: app:doctrine-trust~9864 | The more they afflicted them, the more they grew

=== God our refuge
# from 17760, 17761
--- The Lord is my shepherd
kicker: God our refuge
big: Psalms 23:4 | mark: I will fear no evil: for thou art with me
p: What the unsaved world uses as a false comfort in death should be a comfort in this life for saved believers. We may be in a low point in our lives, a valley, and feel the shadow of death, but the strength to fear no evil comes from knowing the good Shepherd is with us.
p: *Surely goodness and mercy shall follow me all the days of my life.* Goodness and mercy follow us, today ([[Psalms 23:6]]).

# from 17742, 17232
--- Some trust in chariots
kicker: God our refuge
big: Psalms 20:7 | mark: but we will remember the name of the LORD our God
p: This verse is often quoted, but in practice it can be difficult. We often seek reassurance from our material, wealth and position. In our battles, even if we are seemingly overmatched by our enemy, we are still victorious in God.
p: *The rich man's wealth is his strong city* ([[Proverbs 18:11]]). Humble yourself to recognize, God is the only shelter you need.

# from 16299, 15788, 15149
--- Egypt a broken reed
kicker: God our refuge
big: Ezekiel 29:6-7 | mark: they have been a staff of reed to the house of Israel
p: Egypt represents the old, unsaved life, under bondage to sin. We should look at the world's solutions to our problems the same way. Weak and insufficient. Trusting in Egypt is death ([[Isaiah 30:2-3]]).
p: Asa sought the physicians, not the Lord ([[2 Chronicles 16:12]]). There's nothing wrong with using doctors, medicines, but first go to God in prayer. Those men and things may be the tools with which God heals you, but it is God's mighty hand that we need.

# from 14030, 13632, 13108, 14229
--- The battle is the Lord's
kicker: God our refuge
big: 1 Samuel 17:47 | mark: for the battle is the LORD’s
p: It wasn't David who overcame Goliath, but the Lord. Increased faith doesn't mean relying more and more upon yourself, but rather leaning more and more upon Him. No one would think that 300 men blowing trumpets and smashing pots could rout tens of thousands. But they had already been delivered into Gideon's hands ([[Judges 7:7]]).
p: *Be of good courage, and let us play the men for our people* ([[2 Samuel 10:12]]). Too often today, professing Christian men come off as afraid. That's the secret to winning. To win souls and obey God, live righteously and trust in Him when the battle comes.

# from 14535, 15329, 17805
--- The still small voice
kicker: God our refuge
big: 1 Kings 19:12 | mark: after the fire a still small voice
p: The triumph that should have led to revival brought depression and fear in the prophet. But the Lord corrects him: *Yet I have left me seven thousand in Israel* ([[1 Kings 19:18]]).
p: Even after great triumphs of faith, it isn't unusual to feel a sense of letdown. To feel alone. But there is always a righteous remnant. God is not in the wind. He is in the still small voice, heard in our spirit, and He has work for us to do.

# from 14751, 15167, 15474
--- Yet I will rejoice
kicker: God our refuge
big: Habakkuk 3:17-18 | mark: Yet I will rejoice in the LORD, I will joy in the God of my salvation
p: This is a great illustration of the way we, as saved believers, should approach the Great Tribulation should it come in our day, or even individual troubles. In the midst of days, the Lord remembers His mercy.
p: Jehoshaphat called the people together in fast, prayer and singing songs in praise of God, and the Lord confounded their enemies ([[2 Chronicles 20:21-22]]). When we are at the end of our rope, the Lord hears us. Even at the depths of the sea ([[Jonah 2:2]]).

# from 7342, 3474, 17482
--- Never alone
kicker: God our refuge
big: 2 Kings 6:17 | mark: the mountain was full of horses and chariots of fire round about Elisha
p: Anyone who has been a saved Christian for any meaningful length of time will testify that the Lord places angels over us, protecting us, often from ourselves ([[Genesis 32:1-2]]; [[Hebrews 1:14]]).
p: Two are better than one ([[Ecclesiastes 4:9-10]]). With Christ, we are never alone. We have the Comforter ([[John 14:16]]). So, *be of good cheer: for I believe God, that it shall be even as it was told me* ([[Acts 27:25]]).
